WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.760
I’ll be going into spoilers since Avatar
the Way of Water has been out for a couple

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.880
of weeks, so we can talk about the story in
detail.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.370
Obviously, the biggest draw for this movie
is the visual side of production and that

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.660
is all outstanding.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.299
I’m sure you’ve heard about that ad-nauseam
and there’s not much to add in that regard.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.869
So despite looking at this movie from a negative
perspective, I don’t hate it by any means

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:23.630
and just want to open up a discussion about
the story.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.820
The reason why I want to focus on the story
of Avatar: The Way of Water is because this

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.890
movie has been in development for over 10
years.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.791
So surely in that time, the script should
be pretty rock solid, but that didn’t feel

00:00:34.791 --> 00:00:36.410
like the case when watching it.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.130
From there being rough dialogue, weird and
new story mechanics, some baffling plot points,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:45.610
and an overall script that gives off the feeling
of James Cameron having free reign and no

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.980
higher-ups having the balls to address different
issues within the script.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.050
I’ll bring up some examples for all these
points in a second and before doing that,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.930
I want to draw a comparison to the first Avatar.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.440
People have famously called the movie unoriginal
and was just Pocahontas but with Aliens.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.880
However, because of that simple and effective
plot, it translated really well into this

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.330
new setting, and because of this, the first
Avatar wasn’t held back by the story.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.900
This is where I think one of the major components
of Avatar the Way of Water feels off to me.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.380
Because in the first movie, we had a compelling
protagonist go through a riveting adventure

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.829
laced with whimsical and exciting world-building
while being anchored by a strong love story.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:29.450
Along with having a feeling of escapism through
Jake leaving his dour life on earth where

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:34.070
he can’t walk to a vibrant life on Pandora
in which he can transcend anything he could

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.259
do on earth.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:37.159
It was very liberating in a way.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:41.750
In the Way of Water, I feel like we are missing
this strong throughline that carries the movie.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.370
The substitution here is Jake’s family,
but I don’t think it’s as strong as the

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.619
wonder-like awe the first Avatar was able
to capture.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:52.479
That’s kind of here with the beautiful visuals
but not narratively.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:56.149
The characters that we follow have very little
character development and the main emphasis

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:57.649
is on the spectacle.

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:02.649
To me, this creates a feeling of disconnection
because instead of having a symbiotic relationship

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:07.009
between dazzling visuals and good character
work, we instead have minimal character work

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:09.849
with a stronger emphasis on visuals.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:13.569
During certain segments of the movie, it felt
like I was watching an hour-long version of

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:16.180
the ride Flight of Passage in Animal Kingdom.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.640
It was more like an experience, rather than
an engrossing story.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.061
And again, that experience was very fun, but
you don’t have to sacrifice the story and

00:02:24.061 --> 00:02:28.540
just have scenes solely there for the sake
of spectacle, instead, you can do both like

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.319
in the first Avatar.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:34.480
Another thing is that Jake Sully and Neytiri
take a back seat, and Neytiri more than jake

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.910
in that regard, so that their kids can take
center stage.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.941
Now this would be fine in theory if the kids
were as interesting as Jake and Neytiri were

00:02:41.941 --> 00:02:43.180
in the first movie.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.450
To me, this isn’t the case.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.990
Firstly, going back to the bad dialogue, Jake’s
kids are talking as if they are some weird

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:53.659
human kids who say Bro and Cuz in every other
sentence.

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:57.460
This was very jarring, to say the least, and
it doesn’t give off the notion that these

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.790
are Navi.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.010
Now I know that they have a human father figure,
but I find it weird for Jake to teach his

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.470
Navi children human slang words, that not
even kids use that often.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:12.380
It feels like James Cameron is out of touch
with how kids sound, let alone alien kids.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.260
You’d think that Neytiri would want them
to be more skewed toward Navi culture and

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.560
would probably dislike that.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.819
Then moving away from the dialogue, the kids
in most scenes didn’t come across as that

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:23.819
likable.

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:28.319
The two brothers had an extremely basic older/younger
brother dynamic and were constantly coming

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:29.770
across as arrogant.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.970
That’s fine at times but when they are putting
themselves in mortal danger to the point where

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.590
they have to be saved in every other scene
then it becomes annoying narratively.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.920
Like the kids were taken prisoner on like
3 separate occasions.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.510
Once in the first act and then twice while
on the boat.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.140
There was a point in the end where they were
like we have to intervene and they take their

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:51.110
youngest sibling with them for literally no
reason.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:55.890
I was extremely confused by that decision
because she offers no value and they are unnecessarily

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.180
putting more of their family in danger.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.959
The two child characters that I thought were
more interesting were Spider and Kiri.

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:05.790
For Kiri it was kind of hard for me to get
over the fact that she was being played by

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:10.670
a 73-year-old Sigourney Weaver, but she never
came across as overly annoying.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.760
The mechanic where she can control certain
parts of the world of Pandora was a cool direction

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:15.900
to take her.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.329
Then the inherent nature of Spider’s dynamic
of being a human raised by the Navi was fascinating.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:25.870
Then to have him be captured by his resurrected
Avatar father Quaritch, throws on another

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.389
complex layer that in turn makes for some
good scenes.

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:33.550
The only issue here plot-wise for this character
is at the end of the movie.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.110
Throughout the course of Spider’s journey,
he sees how ruthless and cruel Quaritch is

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.419
to the Navi.

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:42.139
There are some brief moments where he kind
of bonds with his father and opens up a bit

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:46.979
with him, but that gets drastically overshadowed
later on by the horrifying things Quaritch

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:48.229
does.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:52.300
Also it feels pretty underdeveloped how Neytiri
was so quick to kill Spider during the hostage

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.240
negotiation in the beginning.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.160
The writers kind of drop it and don’t address
it again.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.750
Seems like this would be something that would
heavily affect Spider’s loyalty.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:05.039
So the ending in which Spider saves Quaritch’s
life seems pretty confusing overall and it

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:08.449
feels like he’s dooming the fate of the
Navi in the process.

00:05:08.449 --> 00:05:11.220
Despite the movie giving off the notion that
he’s still loyal to the Navi.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.620
I’m sure a lot of people face-palmed during
this moment and it feels like James Cameron

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.560
wants to keep this villain character around.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.009
Although, this will kind of be getting into
the wonky new story mechanics because in theory,

00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:26.630
wouldn’t they be able to endlessly bring
back these characters in alien avatars?

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:30.560
Given that they can now suddenly through a
retcon, download the minds of human beings

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.160
as if this is Altered Carbon suddenly.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.130
Seems like a crazy macguffin to just breeze
over pretty quickly in the beginning.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:40.280
Like they can deadass just copy anyone’s
mind now.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.470
What the hell is the point of this dumb orange
goo that they are mining from the brains of

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:47.020
tulkun that halts your aging when you can
just be cloned?

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.860
Which the orange goo is also insanely absurd
that an entire vial is only worth 80 million

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:52.860
dollars.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.060
We live in a world currently where there are
ultra billionaires, let alone the future where

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:01.400
these people will become even more obscenely
rich.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.990
Like Bill Gates can buy about thirteen hundred
of those vials right now and can basically

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.760
use them as his morning routine face lotion.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.669
So that new mechanic was really sill, but
I don’t see the point of this.

00:06:12.669 --> 00:06:17.120
If humans in this story can make Avatars of
aliens, then shouldn’t they be able to do

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.510
so for humans?

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.120
I would assume that would be at least easier.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.620
So then, what would be the point of buying
age lotion when you can transfer your mind

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:25.620
into a new body?

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.370
I know this is something relatively minor
in the grand scheme of things to get caught

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:33.770
up on, but these world-building story elements
don’t add up, and when your world doesn’t

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.360
consistently make sense, then it will come
across as faulty.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.919
This wasn’t as much of an issue in the first
Avatar because it didn’t have conflicting

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:43.069
elements.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:47.250
Unobtanium was kind of a meme but it worked
as a baseline for the antagonists to pursue.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.490
However, in The Way of Water, being able to
easily clone people and copy their minds contradicts

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:55.210
the need for the orange glob of de-aging.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:59.860
This is a big problem to me because this is
the main objective of the antagonists.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:04.600
Then going into some other weird plot points
is that the humans now want to colonize Pandora.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.270
This sounds pretty ridiculous because firstly
why would humans pick a planet with no oxygen

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:13.990
and that also has a hostile population that
they will have to fully exterminate to live

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:14.990
in?

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.110
Wouldn’t it be easier to pick a different
planet at this ponit?

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:21.110
Like it made sense in the first movie to go
to Pandora because it was rich in unobtanium

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:25.449
and they were just there to mine it, but to
choose to live there seems like an odd choice.

00:07:25.449 --> 00:07:30.569
It just feels like James Cameron is forcing
the humans to come back on very slim and unrealistic

00:07:30.569 --> 00:07:34.270
motivations, just so that we could have this
conflict.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:38.360
So reworking their motivations and reasoning
for being there seems like a given and it

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:43.020
can be as simple as there’s no more unobtanium
on earth and they need it on Pandora, and

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:44.669
that’s why they came back.

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:48.830
Not to live there permanently and instead
to essentially wage war on Pandora for all

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:49.830
of its resources.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.830
I know that James Cameron is making a statement
about how the earth is dying and we need to

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:58.360
find a new planet, but when you have the means
of interstellar travel, then surely we’d

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.849
have more options than just Pandora.

00:08:00.849 --> 00:08:05.449
Then to list off a couple of other weird plot
points are the sea people disappearing halfway

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:07.430
through the third act when the eclipse happens.

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:11.840
And then Jake saying that he needs to protect
the forest Navi clan by leaving and then he

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.610
just joins a new clan that he then endangers
with his presence.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.669
Seems like a forced way to get to the sea.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:21.310
I’m also kind of surprised that the humans
didn’t care at all about the forest clan

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.380
and only pursued Jake.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.340
Then one last thing in regard to the script
is that the movie really didn’t need to

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.009
be over 3 hours.

00:08:28.009 --> 00:08:31.740
A lot of the scenes felt pretty extended and
could have been shortened a little.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.990
Although it is really nice to have some breathtaking
visuals to gawk over.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:39.169
It’s just that as I said before, you need
to be more productive and have plot progression

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:41.490
that coincides with the spectacle.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.600
The structure of the movie also feels a little
lopsided because the first act was pretty

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.760
fast-paced in comparison to the long 2nd act.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:53.050
It’s pretty apparent that James Cameron
wanted to get to the sea as soon as possible.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.850
Then there’s one scene that could probably
be deleted in which Quaritch’s team wants

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.610
to tame Mountain Banshees.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.760
The reason why I say this is because they
don’t really utilize them after this because

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.480
they just join the big ship and park them
for the rest of the movie.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:09.260
Quaritch does briefly use it during the final
act against Jake, but you don’t need that

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:11.950
small moment in an already big action set-piece.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.820
This is why I think that the producers were
scared to stand up to James Cameron because

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:19.540
there’s some fluff here that could have
been cut out considering that the movie is

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.709
3 hours and 12 minutes.

00:09:21.709 --> 00:09:24.740
I know I’ve been talking about all of the
negatives within the story but there are still

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:26.640
some shining positives.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.649
My favorite parts are definitely the strong
emotional moments between Jake and Neytiri

00:09:30.649 --> 00:09:32.769
that really carried the movie at times.

00:09:32.769 --> 00:09:37.380
Zoe Saldana in particular really blew me away
with her performance and I’m pretty sad

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.899
that she wasn’t in the movie all that much.

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:42.360
Then the whale poaching was so heartbreaking.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:47.370
The movie really gets you to love the Tulkun
by making them friendly and sentient creatures.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:50.390
Making the aspect of humans hunting them even
more depressing.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:55.980
Definitely, a great element in the story that
was kind of tarnished by the orange goo aspect.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:10:00.010
Overall, the story for Avatar the Way of Water
feels like a step down in comparison to the

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.950
first Avatar which I was kind of surprised
by.

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:06.570
It doesn’t feel as coherent and solid as
the first movie and it felt like the script

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.260
needed a little re-working despite being in
development for so long.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:15.470
But the action was stunning and the VFX will
easily win an oscar with no competition.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:20.000
Despite the lackluster story, I’d still
give it a 3.5 out of 5 on Letterboxd.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.639
Thank you for watching and let me know what
you thought about Avatar: The Way of Water.

